  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1242 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nThis Afternoon through Tonight: Strong/Severe Storm Potential\n\nVisible satellite imagery early this afternoon depicts an upper \nlevel low pressure system across southeastern Manitoba and is \ntrekking off to the southeast. To the south of the low, a weak cold \nfront is racing southeast with westerly to northwesterly winds in \nits wake. Showers and storms continue to form just to the west of \nLake Superior at this time, but cloud cover is decreasing in \ncoverage and temperatures across northeastern Minnesota into western \nWisconsin should begin to rise. The increasing temperatures \nshould lead to increasing instability over the next few hours.\n\nAs the weak surface front interacts with the unstable airmass, \nsynoptic lift and surface lift should be sufficient enough to \ngenerate isolated to scattered storms along and just ahead of the \nfront. Just how far south the storms can generate along the boundary \nremains uncertain, mainly due to lesser instability further south \nalong with higher CIN. There will be sufficient ingredients in place \nfor these storms to become severe, with damaging wind gusts and \nlarge hail being the primary hazards. Given how late the storms are \nforecast to potentially impact the northern counties in the CWA, 7-8 \nPM if not later, nocturnal cooling will increase CIN and a quick \ndecrease in intensity is expected with eastern extent. Shower and \nstorm chances will continue through the overnight hours for areas in \nWisconsin, but the severe threat should quickly decrease after \nsunset.\n\nWednesday through This Weekend: Seasonal with Weekend Rain Chances\n\nForecast models depict upper level ridging should remain to the west \nof the Upper Midwest through much of this forecast period. Cooler \nair will keep temperatures right around average for late August and \nafternoon highs should be in the upper 70s to low 80s each day. As \nthe ridge flattens this weekend, this will open the door for \ndisturbances to round the top of the ridge and they may generate \nshowers and storms across portions of the Northern Plains towards \nthe Great Lakes. The highest chances are Saturday and Sunday for any \nrainfall across the area.",
